\section{Preliminaries}
\label{section:preliminaries}

\subsection{Basic graph terminology}
In this work, all graphs considered are undirected multigraphs (i.e., they may have parallel edges). We use standard graph-theoretic terminology... Let $G=(V,E)$ be a graph. We let $V(G)$ and $E(G)$ denote the vertex-set and the edge-set of $G$, respectively... 

For every two subsets $X$ and $Y$ of $V$, we let $E_G[X,Y]$ denote the set of the edges of $G$ with one endpoint in $X$ and the other endpoint in $Y$...

A \emph{path} $P$ in $G$ is an alternating sequence...
For every two vertices $x$ and $y$ of $G$, we say that $x$ is connected with $y$ if there is a path from $x$ to $y$ in $G$...

Let $G$ be a connected graph. A bipartition $\{X,V\setminus X\}$ of $V(G)$ is called a \emph{cut} of $G$... From now on, the term ``cut" will always mean ``edge-minimal cut". A cut with $k$ edges is called a $k$-cut. We let $\mathcal{C}_\mathit{kcuts}(G)$ denote the collection of all $k$-cuts of $G$...

\subsection{Notation}
...
